(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["principles-of-diagnostic-medicine-i-exam-1-pdm-exam-1-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"PDM I Exam 1 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Principles of Diagnostic Medicine I","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"On a labelled pelvic radiograph, how are bone, gas and a prosthesis described?","choices":["Bone is radiopaque, gas is radiolucent, and the metal prosthesis is very opaque","Bone is radiolucent, gas is radiopaque, and the metal prosthesis is very lucent","Bone is very opaque, gas is radiopaque, and the metal prosthesis is radiolucent","Bone is radiopaque, gas is very opaque, and the metal prosthesis is radiolucent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — one image showing three of the five densities at once. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"What general lesson applies to chemistry results?","choices":["A lab value narrows the differential and directs the next test rather than being diagnostic by itself","A lab value is diagnostic on its own once it falls outside the reference range","A lab value should always be confirmed by imaging before acting","A lab value is only useful when the whole panel is abnormal"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the point-of-care lesson. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 24 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"How should a modality be chosen when the diagnosis is not certain?","choices":["Always order both a plain film and a cross-sectional study at the same visit","Memorise a protocol for each presenting complaint and follow it exactly","Reason from the tissue rather than from a memorised protocol","Always begin with the study carrying the least radiation and escalate only if negative"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — bone points to x-ray or computed tomography; soft tissue often starts with ultrasound. Memorised protocols are fine, but reasoning from the tissue is what generalises. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:10:31"},{"q":"What does the mnemonic VVEEPP stand for?","choices":["Visual acuity, visual fields, external exam, extraocular movements, pupils, pressure","Vision, vessels, eyelids, extraocular movements, pupils, pressure","Visual acuity, visual fields, eyelids, external exam, pupils, photophobia","Visual acuity, vitreous, external exam, extraocular movements, papilloedema, pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the six-step ophthalmic examination. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"In the iron comparison table, which two conditions share a LOW mean corpuscular volume with RAISED ferritin?","choices":["Thalassaemia minor and iron overload","Inflammatory anaemia and iron overload","Thalassaemia major and sideroblastic anaemia","Iron deficiency and inflammatory anaemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — sideroblastic anaemia also shows raised serum iron. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 63 (image only)"},{"q":"You are ordering coagulation studies. Which tube should the specimen arrive in?","choices":["Light blue","Lavender","Gray","Green"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — light blue contains sodium citrate and is the coagulation tube. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"At what hemoglobin is a value low for men and for women?","choices":["Under sixteen grams per decilitre for men, under fourteen for women","Under fourteen grams per decilitre for men, under twelve for women","Under twelve grams per decilitre for men, under fourteen for women","Under thirteen point five grams per decilitre for men, under twelve for women"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — note this differs from the reference table's lower limit of thirteen point five for men. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"How does free hemoglobin reach the urine?","choices":["Through intravascular destruction of red cells","Through failure of tubular reabsorption of protein","Through bleeding anywhere along the urinary tract","Through release from injured skeletal muscle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hemolysis inside the vessels releases it. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How is a posterior-anterior chest film oriented when you look at it?","choices":["As if you were looking down from above, with their right side nearest the top","As if the patient were standing in front of you, with their right side on your left","As if the patient were standing behind you, with their right side on your right","As if you were looking at the patient's feet, with their left side on your right"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the same convention as facing a patient in the room. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which findings does neck computed tomography show?","choices":["Air-fluid levels and sinus opacification","Dermal thickening, increased echogenicity and a cobblestoning pattern","Abscess as a rim-enhancing fluid collection, and enlarged or necrotic lymph nodes","Fat stranding and herniated orbital contents"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the neck column, which extracts out of order from this three-column slide. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A specimen collected two hours ago smells strongly of ammonia. What explains it?","choices":["The sample has been refrigerated","There is a connection between bowel and bladder","The patient is in ketoacidosis","Bacteria broke down the urea as it stood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — standing lets bacteria decompose the urea. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What are the five strengths of computed tomography?","choices":["Calcifications, sinuses, perineural spread, foreign bodies, and skull base disease","Bone, tumours, foreign bodies, vascular thrombosis, and osteomyelitis","Calcifications and bone, sinuses, acute trauma and orbital fractures, foreign bodies, and unstable or claustrophobic patients","Superior soft-tissue contrast, no ionizing radiation, orbital extension, perineural spread, and skull base tumours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — all five as listed. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"In a patient with cystitis, which negative dipstick results help to rule other things out?","choices":["Leukocyte esterase and nitrites","Blood and leukocyte esterase","Glucose, ketones, bilirubin, and protein","Specific gravity, pH, and appearance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — their absence points away from metabolic, hepatic, and glomerular causes. — Lecture recording, 1 September 2026, 49:21"},{"q":"A toddler is being assessed for a urinary tract infection and the nitrite pad is negative. What limits that result here?","choices":["Paediatric specimens must be refrigerated first","Children void too often for the conversion","The pad cannot be used under the age of five","Children produce no urinary nitrates"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the conversion needs urine to sit in the bladder. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which sampling gives higher-yield results in complex wounds such as diabetic foot ulcers and pressure ulcers?","choices":["Blood cultures drawn from two separate sites","Deeper tissue biopsy or aspirate","A superficial swab taken by the Levine method","Repeat superficial swabs taken on consecutive days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — higher yield than a superficial swab in complex wounds. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A patient passes large volumes of very dilute urine with a low specific gravity. Which condition fits?","choices":["Diabetes insipidus","Heart failure","The syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone","Dehydration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — reduced antidiuretic hormone leaves more water in the urine. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"How much of a normal red cell's diameter is central pallor?","choices":["One quarter","Two thirds","One third","One half"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the reference point the hypochromic and hyperchromic definitions depend on. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What are the two causes of isolated hyperbilirubinaemia?","choices":["Gilbert syndrome and haemolysis","Gallstones and primary biliary cholangitis","Viral hepatitis and drug injury","Alcohol and fatty liver disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the two causes of a raised bilirubin with normal enzymes. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 17 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"The blood pad is positive, microscopy shows no red cells, and the serum creatine phosphokinase is raised. Which is it?","choices":["Hemoglobinuria","Hematuria","Contamination of the specimen","Myoglobinuria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a raised creatine phosphokinase points to muscle. — Lecture recording, 1 September 2026, 34:47"},{"q":"A patient has unilateral visual loss. Where does that localise?","choices":["To the optic nerve or the eye itself","To a systemic or intracranial cause","To the optic chiasm","To a retrochiasmal lesion such as a stroke"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — unilateral loss is ocular or optic nerve. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What does an abdominal series evaluate, and how is the patient positioned?","choices":["The gastrointestinal tract, with the patient supine, to assess bowel wall thickness and oedema","The retroperitoneum, with the patient in decubitus, to assess the psoas shadows and the flanks","The gastrointestinal tract, with the patient standing, to assess air-fluid levels or free air","The genitourinary tract, with the patient supine, to assess stones within the collecting system"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — upright is what makes free air and air-fluid levels visible. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A patient presents with an acute febrile illness and suspected septicemia. How should blood cultures be collected?","choices":["A single large-volume sample drawn from either arm before antibiotics are started","Two samples from the same arm, thirty minutes apart","A single sample drawn after the first antibiotic dose","Two separate samples from opposite arms, ideally before starting antibiotic therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and the aerobic sample is drawn first. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Besides reduced kidney clearance, which of these raises blood urea nitrogen?","choices":["Overhydration","Reduced muscle mass","Gastrointestinal bleeding","Low dietary protein intake"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — one of the non-renal causes. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why does splenectomy increase target cells?","choices":["Because the spleen normally produces target cells","Because iron is no longer stored efficiently","Because hemoglobin denatures without splenic clearance","Because of altered filtration dynamics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the spleen normally remodels the membrane as cells pass through. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 43 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Which combination indicates bacterial infection?","choices":["Eosinophils plus basophils","Neutrophils plus eosinophils","Neutrophils plus bands","Lymphocytes plus monocytes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the two together are the bacterial signature. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which is listed as an advantage of point-of-care testing?","choices":["Greater precision than the equivalent central laboratory method in every case","Lower cost than central laboratory testing","Standardised vocabulary across manufacturers","Better care where resources are limited, such as rural settings or disaster zones"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside convenience, speed, less manpower, fewer visits and fingerstick rather than needle stick. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What is recorded on the vertical axis of an audiogram, and what does pure tone audiometry require of the room?","choices":["Sound intensity on the vertical axis, and a soundproof booth in every case","Compliance on the vertical axis, and a quiet environment","Sound intensity on the vertical axis, and a quiet environment","Frequency on the vertical axis, and a quiet environment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — both details from the same slide. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which characteristics describe a screening test?","choices":["Typically inexpensive and easy to perform, indicating whether further testing is needed","Typically invasive with a meaningful risk of complications","Typically performed in order to confirm a condition that is already strongly suspected","Typically reserved for symptomatic patients"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — screening should be performed before more expensive or time-consuming tests. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which characteristic belongs to quantitative testing?","choices":["Results require no instrumentation to interpret","Data are numbers or statistical results, analysed statistically","Data are descriptions requiring non-statistical grouping","Results are always reported as positive or negative"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — quantitative testing measures and tests, answering how many or how much. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which elements should patient education about a diagnostic test include?","choices":["Only the risks, since consent is the legal requirement","The testing process, answers to their questions, and the anticipated timeline for results","Only the preparation instructions, since everything else is better discussed once results return","Only the cost, since that is what most affects the decision"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — all three are named as part of pretest patient and family education. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which two mechanisms control water handling, and therefore serum sodium?","choices":["Thirst and antidiuretic hormone","Renin and angiotensin","Aldosterone and atrial natriuretic peptide","Insulin and the catecholamines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — thirst and antidiuretic hormone. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 9 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"What are the hypo-proliferative causes of normocytic anemia?","choices":["Acute blood loss, intrinsic hemolytic anemia, extrinsic hemolytic anemia and sickle cell anemia","Vitamin B12 deficiency, folate deficiency, alcohol use and liver disease","Iron deficiency, thalassemia, lead poisoning and sideroblastic anemia","Aplastic anemia, anemia of chronic disease, infiltration of bone marrow by tumor, and hypometabolic states"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the four hypo-proliferative causes. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Which non-specific constitutional symptoms are a reason to check a white cell count?","choices":["Pallor, dyspnoea on exertion, palpitations, dizziness and cold intolerance","Unexplained fatigue, weakness, unexplained bruising or bleeding, and weight loss","Fever, chills, body aches and headaches","Night sweats, lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly and pruritus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the four constitutional symptoms as listed. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does poikilocytosis mean?","choices":["Abnormal clumping of red blood cells","Abnormally shaped red blood cells","Abnormal variation in red blood cell size","Abnormal inclusions within red blood cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the umbrella term for the shape variants. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is the other common name for the comprehensive metabolic panel?","choices":["Chem-20","Chem-10","Chem-14","Chem-8"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — chem-14. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which category describes slide examination of a freshly collected specimen by a provider, such as a Gram stain or manual cell count?","choices":["Waived testing","Moderately complex testing","Highly complex testing","Provider-performed microscopy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a distinct complexity category. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is given alongside intravenous iodinated contrast to protect the kidneys?","choices":["One litre of normal saline","One litre of lactated Ringer's","One gram of acetylcysteine","One dose of intravenous furosemide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — one litre of normal saline, for renal protection. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"In the iron transport analogy, what do the home and the percentage saturation represent?","choices":["The home is the red blood cell; the percentage saturation is the reticulocyte count","The home is the bone marrow; the percentage saturation is serum iron","The home is hemosiderin storage, which cannot be measured; the percentage saturation is total iron binding capacity, how many iron atoms can sit on the bus","The home is ferritin, which can be measured directly; the percentage saturation is transferrin, the protein that carries iron in the blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and ferritin is measurable precisely because it is outside the marrow. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 69 (speaker notes)"},{"q":"Which three presentations need no imaging at all?","choices":["Uncomplicated otitis, deep neck infection, and simple abscesses","Uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis, otitis, and simple soft-tissue infections","Uncomplicated acute rhinosinusitis, orbital cellulitis, and simple soft-tissue infections","Otitis, neck mass, and simple soft-tissue infections"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — none of these three needs imaging. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which two structures make up a nephron?","choices":["A calyx and a collecting duct","A glomerulus and a tubule","A renal artery and a renal vein","A cortex and a medulla"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — those two together form the nephron. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"From which material must a Levine specimen not be taken?","choices":["Granulation tissue at the wound base","The wound edge where epithelium is advancing","Any area that has bled during cleaning","Exudate, eschar or necrotic material"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — all three are excluded because they reflect colonisation rather than infection. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Reynolds contrasted panel shorthand with what is written in an electronic medical record. What did she say about abbreviating there?","choices":["Do not abbreviate — write the term out in full","Abbreviate wherever a standard abbreviation exists","Abbreviate only the electrolytes","Follow whatever the ordering laboratory uses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — her rule was that shorthand belongs to handwritten bedside notes, not the record. — Lecture recording, 26 August 2026"},{"q":"What does sensitivity measure?","choices":["The probability that the test is negative when the person does not have the condition","The probability that a person with a positive test has the disease","The probability that a person with a negative test lacks the disease","The probability that the test is positive when the person does have the condition"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — it reflects how well a test detects a condition, and relates to fewer false negatives. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Abnormal liver tests should be followed by which imaging first?","choices":["Plain abdominal radiography","Ultrasound","Computed tomography with contrast","Magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — ultrasound first, for steatosis and biliary dilation. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which posttest activities are grouped under integration and follow-up?","choices":["Monitoring vital signs and administering sedation","Reviewing history and identifying contraindications","Selecting the appropriate collection tube, observing the correct order of draw, and labeling each specimen","Ordering appropriate follow-up laboratory studies, scheduling follow-up, and making appropriate referrals"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, alongside patient education and consideration of emotional well-being. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A patient has free air under the diaphragm. Which history would make perforation NOT the explanation?","choices":["Recent upper gastrointestinal endoscopy without biopsy","A barium swallow performed the previous week","Chronic constipation with a heavy stool burden","Recent laparoscopic surgery, with the gas not yet absorbed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — insufflation of the abdomen puts gas there deliberately. With belly pain, fever, nausea and vomiting and no recent surgery, it is perforation. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:22:55"},{"q":"What does a complete blood count provide information about?","choices":["Clotting function and platelet activity","The hematologic system and other organ systems","The hematologic system only","Bone marrow function only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it reports beyond blood alone. — Complete Blood Count and Hematology Diagnostics - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Renal function must be checked before gadolinium, but for a different reason from iodinated contrast. What is that reason?","choices":["It is a clearance problem — poor function lets it build up in tissue","Gadolinium is far more nephrotoxic than iodinated contrast, so the threshold for withholding it is lower","Gadolinium is cleared hepatically, so renal function predicts the hepatic reserve","Gadolinium interferes with the creatinine assay, so a baseline is needed for comparison"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — gadolinium is not necessarily nephrotoxic itself, but if the kidney cannot clear it, it accumulates in tissue and that accumulation is toxic. With iodinated contrast the agent itself is nephrotoxic. — 2026-08-19 lecture recording, 1:40:24"},{"q":"Besides urinary tract infection, which conditions can turn the leukocyte esterase positive?","choices":["Diabetes insipidus and severe dehydration","Interstitial cystitis and glomerulonephritis","Rhabdomyolysis and muscle injury","Multiple myeloma and amyloidosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — both are inflammatory and can produce pyuria. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"After the puncture site has been disinfected for a blood culture, what must be avoided?","choices":["Drawing the aerobic bottle first","Using the opposite arm for the second sample","Palpating the site, unless sterile gloves are worn","Allowing the antiseptic to dry before puncture"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — palpating after disinfection recontaminates the site. — 1. Principles of Laboratory Diagnostics sv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"How do nitrites come to appear in urine?","choices":["White cells release them as they degranulate","Bacteria reduce urinary nitrates to nitrites","The renal tubules secrete them when acidotic","They are filtered unchanged from the plasma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — bacterial reductase performs the conversion. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"In which patients is ultrasound named as often the first study of choice?","choices":["Adult chest and geriatric patients, and it is also used to screen for fractures","Head-injured and intoxicated patients, and it is also used to exclude a bleed","Oncology and post-operative patients, and it is also used to stage known disease","Female pelvis and paediatric patients, and it is also used to guide procedures"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — no radiation is the reason it leads in both groups. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How is most skin disease diagnosed?","choices":["By culture, with history and inspection used to choose the transport medium","By dermoscopy, with history and inspection used to confirm the reading","By history and visual inspection, with office testing added for uncertain diagnoses","By skin biopsy, with history and inspection used to choose the biopsy site"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — testing supplements the clinical examination rather than replacing it. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Where is sodium the major cation?","choices":["In the extracellular fluid","In the intracellular fluid","In the cerebrospinal fluid specifically","In the biliary and pancreatic secretions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — sodium is the major extracellular cation. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What happens to serum potassium in alkalosis?","choices":["Potassium shifts into cells, so the serum level falls","Potassium shifts out of cells, so the serum level rises","Potassium is retained by the kidney, so the serum level rises","Potassium is unaffected by alkalosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — potassium shifts into cells, so the serum level falls. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Besides buffering, what is bicarbonate's other stated function?","choices":["Maintaining the resting membrane potential across the cell","Determining extracellular osmolality","Helping transport carbon dioxide in the bloodstream","Helping transport oxygen to the tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — buffering pairs with carbon dioxide transport. — 5. Chemistry Panels, Renal Fxn, Elytes.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"At what levels does screening pure tone audiometry present tones?","choices":["Forty to fifty decibels for adults, and thirty to forty decibels for children","Twenty-five to thirty decibels for adults, and fifteen to twenty decibels for children","Fifteen to twenty decibels for adults, and twenty-five to thirty decibels for children","Zero to ten decibels for adults, and ten to fifteen decibels for children"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the upper limits of normal hearing, set lower for children. — 3. svDerm, ENT, Ophtho.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"How are radiographic projections named?","choices":["In the direction the patient faces, from the side nearest the film to the side furthest away","In the order the structures appear, from the most superficial down to the deepest tissue","In the position of the detector, from the cassette's near edge to its furthest corner","In the direction the beam travels, from what it strikes first to the most distal portion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — so posterior-anterior means the beam enters the back and exits the front. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"On a T2-weighted image, how does tissue with high water content appear?","choices":["Dark, and the examples given are fat, oedema, infection, blood and cerebrospinal fluid","Bright, and the examples given are cortical bone, tendon, ligament and calcified plaque","Dark, and the examples given are cortical bone, tendon, ligament and calcified plaque","Bright, and the examples given are fat, oedema, infection, blood and cerebrospinal fluid"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — remember T2 as the sequence where water is white. — 2. svPrinciples of Medical Imaging.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What happens to the fluid and waste that the tubules do not reabsorb?","choices":["It is broken down by tubular enzymes","It is stored in the renal cortex","It returns to the glomerulus to be filtered again","It becomes urine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — whatever is not reabsorbed is excreted as urine. — 6. Urinalysis Diagnostics SV Gopal Fall 2026.pptx, Slide 15"}]};
